WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.389
It's taken far too long to get a Black Widow
solo movie.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:08.000
It took waiting out a real world pandemic
and the character's on-screen death in "Avengers:

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.840
Endgame," but finally "Black Widow" is here.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.950
Confused about some of the plot's twists and
turns?

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.720
Here are the biggest unanswered questions
we have about "Black Widow."

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.769
Early in "Black Widow," director Cate Shortland
uses some clever misdirection.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:26.490
We're led to believe that we're about to get
a showdown between Natasha and Secretary Ross'

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:31.310
men in an office building, when in fact Black
Widow is already long gone -- on a ferry headed

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.750
for a safe house in Norway.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:37.110
While it likely has nothing to do with the
overall story of either "Black Widow" or the

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.500
MCU, it's interesting that Norway is Natasha's
destination, considering who else may be in

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.280
the country when she arrives.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.140
The events of "Black Widow" are set during
2016's "Captain America: Civil War" -- between

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.280
Tony Stark's confrontation with Nat after
the airport battle, and the rescue of the

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.940
anti-registration heroes from the Raft.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.370
What's interesting is that 2017's "Thor: Ragnarok"
also takes place during the events of "Civil

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:05.979
War," and in the beginning of "Ragnarok" we
learn Odin has escaped Loki's magic and is

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:08.659
waiting in Norway for his sons to arrive.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:10.460
Nat isn't in Norway for very long.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.960
It's during her first night in the safe house
that she's attacked by Taskmaster, and soon

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.219
afterward she heads for Budapest.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:21.229
But it's at least possible she was in Norway
at the same time as Odin, perhaps even on

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:22.510
the same day as his death.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:26.469
One of the more mysterious characters of "Black
Widow" is Mason.

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:30.740
It's Mason who secures Nat's safe house for
her, along with a chopper to rescue Alexei

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.080
from prison, and even a quinjet.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.200
There's some palpable sexual tension between
Mason and Nat -- you get the sense either

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.130
they have a romantic past or that they've
been circling each other flirtatiously for

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.140
a long time.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.520
This probably has a lot to do with why Mason
is willing to continue supplying Nat even

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.340
though Secretary Ross is forcing him into
a corner.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.520
While we don't get a lot of details about
Mason in "Black Widow," it's possible we've

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.729
met a relative.

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:59.020
In the comics, Mason is better known as Rick
Mason, aka the Agent.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.899
His father is Phineas Mason, the Tinkerer,
a tech genius known for supplying super villains

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:05.219
with their gadgets.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:10.280
While he's never referred to as the Tinkerer
onscreen, we meet Phineas Mason in 2017's

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.379
"Spider-Man: Homecoming," working for the
Vulture.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:18.700
When we first meet Alexei in 1995, he's clearly
already an adept storyteller considering he's

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.770
been hiding his "family" from the authorities
for years.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.000
"Ha ha ha!

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Still fits!"

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
He's still telling stories just before Nat
and Yelena spring him from prison.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.930
In particular, he enjoys regaling his fellow
prisoners about his supposedly epic battles

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.460
with Captain America.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.120
The one prisoner brave enough to point out
that Steve Rogers was still frozen at the

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.530
bottom of the ocean when Alexei was supposed
to be fighting him pays for it by losing the

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:43.590
use of his hand.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.829
In spite of Alexei's obvious lies, he still
seems to think he and Captain America have

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:50.510
some kind of history, like they did in the
comics.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.439
One of the first things he does after being
sprung from prison is to ask Nat whether or

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:56.660
not Steve Rogers ever asks about him.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.760
Is it possible he actually does have some
kind of history with Captain America?

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.690
On one hand, with all the mystery surrounding
Steve Rogers' journey to the past at the end

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:08.690
of "Avengers: Endgame" -- and the changing
story about whether that takes place in another

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.659
timeline or the prime one -- could he and
a time-traveling Steve Rogers have clashed

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:13.659
at some point?

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:17.830
On the other hand, could Red Guardian be using
some poetic license with a battle that took

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.090
place not with Steve Rogers, but another super
soldier?

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.069
Perhaps Isaiah Bradley or someone like him?

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.519
It's been clear ever since her interrogation
of Loki in "The Avengers" that the innocent

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:31.159
lives Natasha took while working for the Red
Room still haunt her.

00:03:31.159 --> 00:03:35.370
But while we don't hear much about it once
the scene is over, it seems likely Nat and

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.590
her sister took a lot more innocent lives
rescuing their adoptive father from prison.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.659
While defending herself from the prison's
guards, Yelena unintentionally triggers an

00:03:43.659 --> 00:03:44.659
avalanche.

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:48.659
Nat and Alexei narrowly avoid being swept
away by the avalanche, but we never learn

00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:51.550
the fate of the prison's inmates or guards.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.590
Considering the severity of the avalanche
and the fact that the heroes were the only

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:58.650
ones to have a helicopter they could use to
get away, it seems pretty likely most -- if

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.780
not all -- were buried beneath it and killed.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.069
And strangely, this is never mentioned.

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:07.530
Neither Nat, Yelena, nor Alexei betray any
kind of regret over what happens.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.170
There are a lot of differences between the
Taskmaster of "Black Widow" and the villain

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.950
of the source material, including gender.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.560
But one thing that remains the same is their
abilities.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.060
In both the comics and the movie, Taskmaster
can instantly perform any martial task they

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.290
see performed by combat experts.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:28.720
In the film, we see Taskmaster can use a bow
and arrow like Hawkeye, a shield like Captain

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.300
America, and even claws like Black Panther.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:34.009
We also see Taskmaster using a sword.

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:38.130
The thing is, at this point in the MCU we
haven't seen any major heroes or villains

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.550
using swords -- at least none Taskmaster would
have access to recordings of.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.110
Of course, it isn't like you have to be a
superhero or villain to use a sword.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.590
Still, almost all the other forms of martial
combat we see Taskmaster use come from some

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.770
major hero.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.810
It could be that this is foreshadowing for
the upcoming "Eternals," which will include

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:59.970
the premiere of the Ebony Blade-wielding Black
Knight played by Kit Harington.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.880
Of course, another Marvel hero famous for
wielding swords is the vampire hunter Blade,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.220
who Mahershala Ali is set to play.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.650
We know for a fact parts of "Eternals" will
be set in the MCU's past, and it's possible

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.570
part of "Blade" could be as well.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:18.070
If recordings of either heroes exist, it could
explain Taskmaster's own skill with a blade.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:23.210
We get teased with the possibility of learning
about Natasha's birth parents toward the end

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.889
of "Black Widow."

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:28.740
Dreykov acts as if he's going to give Nat
her mother's name, then cruelly says only

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.860
that her headstone reads "Unknown."

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.139
Shortly before her death in "Endgame," Nat
gets the tiniest of crumbs from the Red Skull.

00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:39.430
He calls her "Natasha, daughter of Ivan,"
and moments later we learn that -- until the

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:43.740
name came out of the Skull's mouth -- Nat
didn't know so much as his first name, much

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.020
less his last.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.820
All she's able to learn from Melina is that
the story she was told about her mother abandoning

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:49.910
her was a lie.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:54.250
Sadly, with Nat dying in "Endgame," it seems
unlikely we'll ever learn anything about her

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:55.669
birth parents.

00:05:55.669 --> 00:06:00.080
Since Yelena appears set to continue to be
a part of the MCU going forward, it could

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.880
be that she'll investigate further, but we'd
assume she'd be much more interested in learning

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.629
about her own origins.

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:09.380
And maybe finally getting to the bottom of
"The Pose."

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.379
"Why do you always do that thing?"

00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:12.379
"What thing?"

00:06:12.379 --> 00:06:13.379
"The pose!

00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:14.379
It's a fighting pose.

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:15.379
You're a total poser."

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.949
We don't actually see much of Dreykov before
the end of the film, but if one thing is clear,

00:06:20.949 --> 00:06:22.419
the guy likes to brag.

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:26.690
With what he thinks is a helpless Natasha,
Dreykov opens up about his global network

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.819
of mind-controlled Widows and the kind of
power they give him.

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:31.539
The scene feels a lot like the A.I.

00:06:31.539 --> 00:06:36.110
Zola bringing Steve and Nat up to speed on
Hydra and Bucky in "Captain America: The Winter

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:40.720
Soldier," but with one exception -- we never
get any solid idea of exactly what the Widows

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.819
have done.

00:06:41.819 --> 00:06:45.169
Dreykov gives some very broad strokes about
what he can do with the Widows.

00:06:45.169 --> 00:06:49.020
He makes the kind of boasts you'd expect -- that
with them he can topple governments, he can

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:50.830
destroy economies, and so on.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.940
And it's clear that, yes, for a while now
the emissaries of the Red Room have spilled

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:56.319
plenty of blood.

00:06:56.319 --> 00:07:00.599
But we never learn exactly what kind of power
moves Dreykov has made with the Widows.

00:07:00.599 --> 00:07:01.840
Has he toppled governments?

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.460
Has he destroyed economies?

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.270
Exactly how does the world of the MCU look
different because of what he's made his Widows

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:08.270
do?

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.129
It turns out all the Widows, including Nat,
have been conditioned to be unable to directly

00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:14.810
attack Dreykov while in range of his pheromones.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:19.350
Nat pretends to be surprised by this, but
we learn she and Melina worked it out earlier.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:23.789
Melina tells her she will need to completely
sever a nerve connected to her olfactory system

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:27.860
-- essentially losing her sense of smell to
bypass Dreykov's pheromones.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.460
Nat tries to get Dreykov to do it himself
by provoking him into beating her, but when

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.819
he can't finish the job, she does it for him.

00:07:34.819 --> 00:07:38.659
So does this mean Nat is completely without
her sense of smell for the rest of her life?

00:07:38.659 --> 00:07:43.509
She painfully resets her broken nose after
Dreykov escapes, but severing a nerve sounds

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:45.320
fairly, well, permanent.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.699
It doesn't sound like something that could
be corrected by resetting a broken nose.

00:07:49.699 --> 00:07:54.129
By the end of "Black Widow," all of the surviving
Widows are freed from Dreykov's control.

00:07:54.129 --> 00:07:58.610
Before leaving Dreykov's office, Nat downloads
the data about other Widows across the globe

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.319
and she hands it off to Yelena.

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:05.039
Alexei, Melina, and Yelena leave with all
the Widows, including Taskmaster.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:08.370
So, what are the Widows and the rest of Nat's
found family doing now?

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:13.680
In most likelihood they spent some time locating
and liberating the rest of Dreykov's Widows,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.350
but the present day MCU is set in 2023, seven
or so years after "Black Widow."

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:23.720
We know from the post-credits scene that Yelena
is working for Contessa Valentina Allegra

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.729
de Fontaine, aka Val.

00:08:25.729 --> 00:08:27.099
"Don't call me that."

00:08:27.099 --> 00:08:28.099
"Copy."

00:08:28.099 --> 00:08:32.420
Val, who we were introduced to in "The Falcon
and the Winter Soldier," seems like she would

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:36.149
certainly love to get the rest of the Widows
on her payroll, but that doesn't mean the

00:08:36.149 --> 00:08:37.979
Widows would feel the same.

00:08:37.979 --> 00:08:42.050
Perhaps some went to work for Val, some became
freelancers, and maybe others did their best

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:43.820
to build "normal" lives.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.950
Then there's the question of what happened
to Alexei and Melina.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.660
They clearly still share a mutual attraction,
so there's a decent chance they're a couple.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.320
As for what else they're up to -- considering
their former employers, they probably don't

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.080
have a ton of moral scruples regarding who
they do and don't work for.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:01.610
They might have found a place working for
Val as well.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:06.790
For fans of Marvel's trademark post-credits
scenes, "Black Widow" doesn't disappoint.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:10.529
It's clear Yelena has been working for Val
for a while, and the Contessa has come to

00:09:10.529 --> 00:09:15.340
give Yelena her next target, who she refers
to as "the man responsible for your sister's

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.839
death" — in other words, Clint Barton.

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:21.720
Anyone who's seen "Avengers: Endgame" knows
saying Barton is responsible for Natasha's

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.800
demise is a pretty limited, and obviously
manipulative, interpretation of Natasha's

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:27.800
sacrifice.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.079
Regardless, what's most intriguing here is
the question of why Val wants Hawkeye dead.

00:09:32.079 --> 00:09:36.230
Without knowing more about Val and her agenda,
the most obvious answer is that it has something

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:41.350
to do with Barton's vigilante activities as
Ronin in the years between the events of "Avengers:

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:43.160
Infinity War" and "Endgame."

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:47.570
It could be that the government now sees him
as a wild card and wants him taken out quietly,

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:51.450
or it could be that the carnage he caused
in the global crime community made the wrong

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:52.760
people angry.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:55.931
It seems likely we'll have to wait until the
release of Disney+'s "Hawkeye" to know for

00:09:55.931 --> 00:09:56.931
sure.

00:09:57.931 --> 00:10:01.980
For fans hoping that the MCU is building its
own big-screen Thunderbolts -- a Marvel Comics

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.990
team composed of mostly either reformed villains
or heroes with criminal pasts -- the post-credits

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.860
scene of "Black Widow" offers some promising
teases.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.560
The very fact that Val appears in the scene
is a hint.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.490
She's introduced in "The Falcon and the Winter
Soldier," a series which had long been rumored

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:20.529
to build the foundation for the Thunderbolts'
entry into the MCU.

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:24.310
Along with featuring Bucky and Zemo -- both
of whom have fronted their own versions of

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:29.481
the Thunderbolts in the comics -- the series
sees John Walker recruited by Val, and considering

00:10:29.481 --> 00:10:34.380
Walker's actions in the series, he certainly
rates as a guy who could use some redemption.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.970
Val's meeting with Yelena can only add more
fuel to the speculation.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:42.190
Not only would an assassin like Yelena be
a perfect fit for an MCU Thunderbolts, but

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:46.540
she's tasked with taking out Hawkeye, yet
another character who leads his own Thunderbolts

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:47.550
team in the comics.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:51.760
We'll have to wait and see, obviously, but
the more Val shows up, the more likely it

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.120
seems she's planting seeds for a team that
isn't quite as clean-cut as the Avengers.

